Molly McHugh has joined us on the  program before she is back today she is  a foreign policy and strategy consultant  also an information warfare expert she  had a really interesting article article  in Politico actually about the release  the memo hashtag which we've been  talking about quite a bit and Molly what  what you reported is that there actually  was significant bot activity behind that  memo and from the beginning it appeared  to me to be a very concerted sort of  distraction technique actually from the  substance of the of the FBI  investigation by robert muller talk to  us about how you first started following  this um well you know obviously we  started paying attention to it our team  here at new media frontier our new  company and we started paying attention  to this because it was being amplified  so quickly and it went from being  nowhere to being everywhere at the same  time and how that happens there's always  a really interesting process I think  there were some initial reports people  saying it's Russian bots pushing this  hashtag or it's something else pushing  this hashtag and and what that actually  is and how it happens it's much more  interesting process to begin to which is  why we started looking at it and I think  we lay out in the little go piece pretty  clearly that it's actually a blend of a  variety of different elements that came  together to make this campaign work  effectively there were Russian bots  there were other BOTS there were sort of  far-right information networks in the  u.s. engaged in these campaigns some of  the accounts are automated some of the  accounts are people some of the accounts  or send me automated so it's sort of a  layering on techniques and and tactics  to take a topic sort of organize a bunch  of a bunch of things behind it's the  same campaign and then target it at law  makers and others who are going to be  making decisions on this topic and so  it's sort of those three factors the  amplification the targeting and the  organization that were really effective  and how this worked how the how these  elements came together and worked very  effectively and achieving the outcome  they desired  can you talk a little bit about the  targeting piece  so a lot of the initial tweets that  started from sort of a very low level  network on Twitter we're tagging  Republican congressman either from the  House Intelligence Committee or some of  the more sort of pro-trump White House  defender voices on the hill so people  like that gets the Zeldin some were  tagging Paul Ryan as well so people who  were who were involved in lobbying their  Republican colleagues and and themselves  having to vote within the Intelligence  Committee on releasing the memo and  we're being heavily targeted by these  messages on social media so hundreds of  thousands of times over a week a week  and a half this was an active campaign  before the committee voted and that is  essentially an effective form of  influence and lobbying in terms of  getting a message in front of an elected  official and then getting them to act on  that information when we first mentioned  that there was a lot of bot activity  around this hashtag I would get sort of  anecdotal emails from people saying  David I used the hashtag and I was not  part of either a Russian coordinated  effort nor people lobbying members of  Congress or whatever else can you talk a  little bit about how maybe the term  useful idiot applies here and again  that's not an ad hominem that's actually  a term that has a very very specific  meaning where these campaigns don't  actually require the sort of  conspiratorial knowledge of all  participants to be effective no that's  that's exactly right and I think that  that's where these echo chambers are  really effective there is behind and  underneath a lot of what we see online  there is a lot more organization and  purposefulness that I think we often  admit there are on the far right and the  far left but more so on the far right  had been documented and discussed a lot  these sort of rooms and chat groups and  and sort of other organizational tools  that are being used by far right and all  right interests in the United States to  coordinate their messaging campaigns and  build them online and that does exist  but that's not to say everything is  behind  a puppet master you know once people are  have established networks on Twitter  they promote things from people they  know and trust  same on Facebook same on other social  media so once you build these structures  it's far easier to introduce information  to them and and get them to move in the  places that you want them to and I think  sometimes people just aren't aware of  how they are engaged in this however  their there are a lot of people that are  aware of what they're doing and the  combination of those things of real  people's accounts who are basically  semi-automated acting like human bots  themselves so their accounts  automatically follow and retweet and  move content from specific accounts from  specific narratives so you have accounts  of real people who were tweeting  released the memo a hundred times in a  day that is not normal behavior for any  one social media account so they're sort  of knowingly and purposefully acting and  saying we need to promote this to make  this trend and that is an interesting  element of the different pieces that we  were looking at because they're sort of  the human automation / amplification  side and the straight sort of bought  actual automated you know scripted by  algorithms side and the combination of  those two is very effective and the goal  of both of them is essentially to build  these trends to target people that will  see them and to get that information  into and promoted from verified accounts  because once you hit sort of get a  verified account to promote these on  Twitter can you talk a little bit about  the the sort of function of analytical  software to look through the usage and  the sort of pattern of usage of the  hashtag because people might hear you  and I talking now and they'll say ok it  must have you know Mollie McHugh  scrolling through a Twitter feed  manually is not going to give us  anything beyond sort of anecdotal  information but we're talking about the  use of actual analytical software to  examine this hashtag  are we not yeah absolutely and and we  have a combination  software that we're building ourselves  plus some of the commercially available  tools and some of it is sort of free  online things to do this but it's a we  looked at this in a variety of ways some  of it was scrolling through specific  accounts in painful detail trying to  figure out who they were what they were  where they came from  etc but you also have to look at the  volume over time a number of accounts  engaged and these campaigns how they  acted together sort of where the  information comes from where it gets  injected to how it gets promoted  ultimately over time and and that is  there's a variety of ways to do that the  software that visualized those things in  different ways to pull data from  different places but in particular for  this campaign it was useful for us to  have access to some of the stored  Twitter data there's a lot of the camp a  lot of the accounts that were engaged in  this campaign have either been deleted  or suspended or have deleted their own  content on the release the memo hashtags  since the memo was released which is  also interesting so you kind of need  these different elements coming together  to look at this and I think that one of  the best examples of this was there was  a bike a blogger or something but who  had published his own analysis of the  origins of release the memo but he got  it wrong because he didn't have access  to the information from the account that  have been released or blocked and and  that was important to have to be able to  look at the beginning of the campaign in  sort of layman's terms I think that this  succeeded in the sense that it did  significantly pull attention away from  the sort of subject matter of Robert  Moeller's investigation but also from  other stories that were not particularly  positive stories for Donald Trump and  for several days it really got a lot of  attention but that's just sort of me  taking like a generalist perspective how  would you assess the success of this  campaign in a variety of different ways  it was extremely successful and I think  in the base at the most basic level the  goal of establishing these types of  information networks through social  media or through other means is building  and using activation potential and in  this case it worked very effectively  you have  an extremely engaged community pushing  this narrative pushing this idea it  doesn't ultimately matter what the memo  was or what was in it I don't think it  changed anybody's mind about it one way  or the other but it was the the  organizing power of the idea of released  the memo has now created a very  comprehensive and detailed conspiracy  architecture into which a lot of the  narratives currently being pushed from  the same lanes make a lot of sense you  know the FBI is bad US intelligence is  bad the u.s. is surveilling its own  people this is bad and all of this kind  of fits into the talking points that you  hear coming unfortunately from the Oval  Office and from other right media in the  US but this one hashtag became kind of  organizing principle in people's heads  for for how this made sense so it was  extremely effective variety of ways how  else might we measure it in other words  are there any numerical comparisons that  can be made or what what else do you  look at when you're sort of looking at  the spread of the hashtag well there  were a couple of things that we were  using to look at how widespread or  effectiveness was one was on the  targeting East for Congress and I think  the the volume there spoke it said a lot  there were hundreds of thousands of  tweets targeting members on the  committee before they voted to release  the memo and if you compare that to sort  of a very active period when there would  have been constituents like actual  people calling into congressional  offices about health care about you know  the tax bill about some folks it's  happening that people want to have their  voices heard on this was you know three  times that amount of volume per day in  some cases in terms of how many messages  an individual member would have been  getting so the perception for them would  have been this is something that has a  tremendous amount of support behind it  even if they don't know what the origins  of that are there there are Twitter  mentions and timelines would have been  totally flooded by this concept and that  does make an impression on how people  think about these things the other thing  we tried to compare the release the memo  campaign to two other sort of similarly  timed events that were actually  happening the 2018 women's March and NFL  playoff game that was that week  and if you look at the total volume of  tweets that were coming from over a  similar period of time so about eight  hours of time if you look at the total  volume of tweets coming from what we  know were around women a million women  and other participants engaging in these  demonstrations and then a lot of people  watching a very popular national  football event in both cases those were  you know a third or less a third or less  of the volume of tweets as the release  the memo hashtag was getting when it was  being promoted and I think that's that's  really significant that that initial  that initial ramped up phase was so  quick but it was happening late at night  but it was happening primarily without  the engagement of some of the big  amplifiers that we all know know Hannity  people like that they were engaged very  late in the campaign this was coming  from other places or the big voices got  involved checkout Molly McHugh's article  in Politico how Twitter BOTS and Trump  fans made release the memo go viral  Molly thanks as always for talking to us  thanks for having me on

